WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.640
So in programming, there's
three sort of simple ways

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:05.850
that you can add control
flow to your programs.

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:08.400
And that's making one
decision and choosing

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.910
whether to execute something
or execute something else.

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:16.740
The first is a simple if.

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:19.500
And given a program
that just linearly

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.410
has statements
that get executed,

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:26.570
whenever I reach
an if statement,

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:28.867
you're going to
check the condition.

00:00:28.867 --> 00:00:30.450
The condition is
going to be something

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:33.625
that's going to get evaluated
to either true or false.

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:40.060
So I've reached
the condition here.

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:41.750
And if the condition
is true, then I'm

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:46.430
going to additionally execute
this extra set of expressions.

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:48.080
But if the condition
is false, then I'm

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:50.510
just going to keep going
through the program

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:52.842
and not execute that
extra set of instructions.

00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:59.400
How does Python know which
instructions to execute?

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:03.470
They're going to be inside
this what we call code block.

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:06.609
And the code block is
denoted by indentation.

00:01:06.609 --> 00:01:08.150
So it's going to be
everything that's

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:12.970
indented is part of
that if code block.

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:16.420
Typically, four
spaces is indentation.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:17.440
OK.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:21.890
So that's how you
write code that

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:25.870
decides whether to execute
this extra thing or not.

00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:29.330
Now let's say I don't just
want to execute an extra thing,

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:32.420
I want to reach a
point where I say,

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:37.430
I'll either go down this path
or I'll do something else.

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:42.900
That's this right here.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:49.230
So this if else construct
says this is my code,

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:52.020
I've reached my
decision point here,

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:56.980
if the condition
inside the if is true,

00:01:56.980 --> 00:02:00.150
then I'm going to execute maybe
this set of statements here.

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:05.810
But if the condition
is not true,

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:08.780
then I'm not going to execute
that set of statements,

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:15.710
and instead I'm going to
execute under whatever else is.

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:17.320
So using this
construct, I'm either

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:19.810
going to do one set of
expressions or the other,

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:21.790
but never both.

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:23.630
And after I've executed
one or the other,

00:02:23.630 --> 00:02:25.985
I'm going to continue on with
just the regular execution

00:02:25.985 --> 00:02:26.610
of the program.

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:35.430
OK.

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:37.170
So we're able to either
choose one thing,

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:39.169
choose one thing or
another, but what if we want

00:02:39.169 --> 00:02:42.490
to have more than one choice?

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:46.590
So if some number is equal
to zero, I want to do this.

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:48.450
If it's equal to 1,
I want to do this.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:51.180
If it's equal to 2, I want
to do this, and so on.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:53.940
That's where this
last one comes in.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:03:00.660
And we introduced this other
key word here called elif.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:04.230
So that stands for
short form for else if.

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:08.170
So first we check if
this condition is true.

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:09.810
So we're going
through our program,

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:11.910
we've reached our
decision point,

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:14.820
if the condition is true,
we're going to execute maybe

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:16.200
this set of instructions.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.400
If the condition is
not true, maybe we'll

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:24.470
check-- if the
condition is not true,

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:26.860
we will check this
next condition.

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:29.722
That's part of the
elif right here.

00:03:29.722 --> 00:03:31.180
And if that one's
true, we're going

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:33.640
to execute a different
set of instructions.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:36.184
You can have more than one elif.

00:03:36.184 --> 00:03:37.600
And depending on
which one's true,

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:40.870
you're going to execute a
different set of instructions.

00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:43.390
And then this last else
is sort of a catch all

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:46.430
where if none of the previous
conditions were true,

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:50.020
then just do this last
set of expressions.

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:53.110
So in this case, you're
going to choose between one

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:55.600
of these three-- one
of these four roots,

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:58.076
or however many you have.

00:03:58.076 --> 00:03:59.950
And then when you're
done making your choice,

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:02.751
you're going to execute the
remaining set of instructions.

00:04:06.190 --> 00:04:09.310
So the way this works is if
more than one condition is true,

00:04:09.310 --> 00:04:12.880
you're actually just going
to enter one of them.

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:15.940
And you're going to enter the
very first one that's true.

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:17.860
So you're never going
to enter more than one

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:20.560
of these code blocks.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.140
You always enter one, and
you enter the first one

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:24.810
that evaluates to true.
